  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA more active weather pattern is forecast to take shape beginning \nearly next week as another upper-level system (albeit a more intense \none) approaches the region on Monday. As the system approaches, rain \nchances begin to increase across our westernmost zones. Long-range \nguidance continues to show remnants of Hurricane Polo being pulled \ninto the aforementioned system. This will provide abundant mid-level \nPacific moisture while lee cyclogenesis draws in Gulf moisture at \nthe lower levels. As a result, areawide rain chances can be expected \nbeginning Monday night and lasting through at least Wednesday, with \nthe best chances across southeast New Mexico and far west Texas. \nAnomalously high PWAT values associated with the tropical airmass \nare likely to exceed the daily maximum climatologically speaking, \nwhich means flash flooding is a major concern next week (especially \nin locations where soils are completely saturated due to recent \nrainfall). Guidance continues to indicate Tuesday being the \n\"wettest\" day given the positioning of these two systems as they \nwould provide plenty of lift and moisture. However, there remains \nuncertainty regarding the exact positioning/timing of the \naforementioned systems and their impacts. \n\nAside from the heavy rain/flash flood potential, long-range \nguidance depicts a cold front sweeping through our area sometime \naround Wednesday/Wednesday night next week. This could yield \nafternoon highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s, with drier conditions \nbehind the front late in the week. Though some rain chances (10-30%) \nstick around across our eastern zones as the upper trough departs \nthe region. \n  \nGreening",
